Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog** audit of `OrderProcessorTests.cs`.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 5           | 4              |
| Low      | 1           | 8 (all)        |
| **Total**| **10**      | **8 unique**   |

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10.0 · 9 test methods, 1 class

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (lines 24–39)

```csharp
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
{
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` means only one assertion path ever executes per run. If the production code returns an unexpected `TotalAmount`, the wrong branch silently passes. This hides a testing gap — you are never verifying *both* statuses.

**Fix — split into two deterministic tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_StandardOrder_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_PremiumOrder_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 5) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One status path is never tested; regressions slip through undetected.

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (lines 43–48)

```csharp
public void ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
}
```

**Problem:** No assertions, no expected-exception annotation. MSTest reports this as "passed" even if `ProcessOrder` returns garbage data or corrupts state — as long as no exception is thrown.

**Fix — assert the expected outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_ValidOrder_ReturnsSuccessResult()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage metrics look good but nothing is actually verified.

